% ============================================================================
% igneous-rock-type-eruption-location — for extrusive igneous rock, the
% INDIVIDUAL location(s) an already-quoted NPS sentence lists as where it
% erupts, decoded one-per-row (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `igneous-rock-type.adj`
% (`igneous_rock_type(type, description)`, the two-way intrusive/extrusive
% split by cooling location). That table's own header already quotes,
% verbatim, the extrusive row's defining NPS sentence in full, and it lists
% TWO locations joined by "or" — a fact the single `description` atom folded
% into one compound label, with no room to decode each listed location as
% its own queryable fact:
%
%   extrusive (two alternatives joined by "or"):
%     "Extrusive igneous rocks are erupted onto the surface or into the
%      atmosphere."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? igneous_rock_type_eruption_location(extrusive, $Location)
%       % surface, AND atmosphere (two answers)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `igneous_rock_type_eruption_location(type,
% location)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns each location WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on an igneous type whose own
% cited span names only ONE location with no listed alternative. `intrusive`
% is deliberately NOT a row here: its own already-quoted span ("Intrusive
% igneous rocks solidify within Earth.") names exactly one location — no
% enumerated "or"/list of alternatives to decode — so this sibling is
% HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent, grounding exactly the one igneous type
% whose cited span lists more than one location and abstaining on the
% other, or any other word, rather than inventing one. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a location and recall which igneous type erupts there:
%
%     ? igneous_rock_type_eruption_location($Type, atmosphere)
%       % extrusive
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NPS sentence
% `igneous-rock-type.adj`'s own header already quotes in full for its
% extrusive row — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding each
% listed location as its own row instead of folding the whole clause into
% one compound `description` atom. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `igneous-rock-type.adj` already carries (NPS, a primary U.S. government
% science resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table igneous_rock_type_eruption_location {
    columns type, location

    row (extrusive, surface)
    row (extrusive, atmosphere)

    source "Extrusive igneous rocks are erupted onto the surface or into the atmosphere."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/igneous.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
